## Live-surface isolation

- <live_surfaces> are where the work is used or served, never where it is
  developed: never edit, switch, repoint or experiment directly on one.
  Examples: <live_surface_examples> (a running service, a published document,
  a live mailing list, a shared account).
- Work happens on a separate <working_copy> made from <source_of_truth>, and
  reaches the live surface only through <promotion_route>.
- Before a build, restart, publish, send or promotion, or a claim about what is
  live, resolve the exact target and its current version; a name, port or
  label alone is not proof.

## Data isolation

- Tests, drills, rehearsals and experiments never use <real_data>, such as
  production records, real recipients, real customers or real accounts. They
  use <disposable_data>.
- Before anything that can write, resolve the destination explicitly; an unset
  target that could fall back to the real one is unsafe.
- An authorized change to real data follows <real_data_procedure>: approval,
  backup, verification and rollback.
- Configure the project through its source files and supported surfaces; never
  edit <system_of_record> directly to force a setting.

